    "redSelection": "1 new test failed by the exact unwanted warning; 21 prior cases intentionally skipped; no timeout.",
    "nativeEvidence": "getClientFriendlyName substitutes normalized source clientName, not source getLibraryName. Compiler friendlyName supports arbitrary Type format source, including ModelProperty.",
    "counterexample": "Source.original has @clientName(\"renamed\") and @friendlyName(\"renamed\"); Result.value uses @friendlyName(\"{name}\", Source.original). Source SDK name remains renamed, but Result.value changes renamed to original when selected override is absent."
  },
  "correction": {
    "design": "Factor existing active friendly-name template selection into internal getClientFriendlyNameTemplate, shared by actual SDK resolver and lint. During the existing semantic traversal, compare each actual friendly-name consumer with/without each explicit source ModelProperty clientName application; retain the authored override if any consumer name changes in any relevant scope.",
    "scopeHandling": "Reuse existing global scoped-entry boundaries and semantic authored application scopes. No custom scope parser.",
    "multiplicity": "Dependent effects set the existing authored-node aggregate to meaningful. Subsequent visits cannot reset it to redundant; no new declaration registry, graph, state map or secondary program walk.",
    "cost": "No SDK context/package creation or naming comparison for unrelated consumers. Only active friendly-name templates with ModelProperty sources and explicit clientName applications trigger dependent comparisons. Existing global SDK resolver keeps the original early guards and same template-selection logic.",
    "options": "Native linter uses the established emitter-independent default TCGCContext across language scopes. It does NOT infer SDK-context-specific options from arbitrary emitter configuration. Actual getLibraryName behavior retains the existing emitContext.options false guard; new SDK-package test proves true/false contexts independently. This default-policy scope is now explicit in native docs.",
    "noEffectControls": "A consumer-specific explicit clientName masks the source change; a same-spelling source override does not change template substitution; a scoped exact-name repeat remains redundant when spelling and exact policy stay the same.",
    "stateSafety": "No original Program state mutation; actual no-override SDK controls compile separate programs."
  },
